### TIER C - ARCHITECTURE REGRESSION
### ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# C1: Session key is (channelType, channelId, userId) triplet
# REST sessions use "REST" as channelType, sessionId as channelId,
# and apiKey or userId for user. Telegram uses TELEGRAM + chatId + userId.
# Cross-channel isolation: same chatId on different channel types must
# produce different sessions.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### [C1-SETUP] Create two sessions - both must get distinct IDs
# @name c1SessionA
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/sessions
Content-Type: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

###

# @name c1SessionB
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/sessions
Content-Type: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

###

### [C1-VERIFY] Both sessions must have different IDs
# Inspect: c1SessionA.response.body.sessionId != c1SessionB.response.body.sessionId
# This verifies the registry creates unique entries per call.

POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/sessions/{{c1SessionA.response.body.sessionId}}/run
Content-Type: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}
{"goal": "My session is A.", "agentRole": "coordinator", "maxIterations": 1}

###

POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/sessions/{{c1SessionB.response.body.sessionId}}/run
Content-Type: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}
{"goal": "My session is B.", "agentRole": "coordinator", "maxIterations": 1}

###

### Session A must NOT see Session B's context
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/sessions/{{c1SessionA.response.body.sessionId}}/run
Content-Type: application/json
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}
{"goal": "What session letter am I in?", "agentRole": "coordinator", "maxIterations": 1}

###

DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/sessions/{{c1SessionA.response.body.sessionId}}
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

###

DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/sessions/{{c1SessionB.response.body.sessionId}}
X-Amsha-API-Key: {{validKey}}

###


